# Golden CUDA source is compared byte-for-byte against the emitter's LF output
# (`include_str!` in the contraction byte-identity tests). The emitter always
# writes '\n'; force these goldens to LF on checkout so byte-identity holds on
# Windows too (autocrlf would otherwise convert them to CRLF and fail the test).
#
# Carried over from the Baracuda workspace. The original rule named
# crates/baracuda-kernelgen/src/goldens/*.cu; after the CUDA emitter was carved
# into baracuda-cuda-emit the goldens went WITH it, so that path matches nothing
# here and the protection silently did not apply -- the 32 .cu harnesses under
# ondevice/ landed as CRLF on the first cut and had to be renormalized.
#
# Match every .cu in the workspace instead of one directory, so a future
# relocation cannot quietly drop the guarantee again.
*.cu text eol=lf

# Vendored KISS artifacts are copied VERBATIM from the KISS repository and their
# whole value is that `diff` against the source is meaningful — that is the only
# thing separating a vendored file from a retyped one. With autocrlf=true a
# fresh checkout would materialize them as CRLF while KISS's originals are LF,
# so every line would differ and the refresh procedure in kiss/README.md would
# report a change on a byte-identical file.
#
# A verifier that cries wolf is worse than no verifier: it teaches the operator
# to skip the diff, and the next real divergence goes through.
#
# Matched by directory rather than by file, per the lesson recorded above — a
# rule naming one path stops protecting anything the moment the file moves, and
# does so silently.
crates/unpopped-vocab/kiss/** text eol=lf
